# MILESTONE 2: Stylesheet System

**Strategic goal**: Add CSS-style theming so users can design large plugins with consistent appearance, and so changing one style updates dozens of components automatically.

**Why this is second**: After living with the live JUCE bridge, the natural next pain point is "I have 40 knobs and they all look slightly different - I want one place to control them." This is exactly what CSS solves for web developers, and it's what PluginGuiMagic provides for JUCE.

## 2.1 The Stylesheet Format

Define a CSS-inspired format that lives inside the design XML:

```xml
<stylesheet>
  <variables>
    <var name="accent" value="#ffaa33"/>
    <var name="panel-bg" value="#2a2825"/>
    <var name="knob-size-large" value="64"/>
    <var name="knob-size-medium" value="48"/>
    <var name="knob-size-small" value="32"/>
    <var name="font-label" value="Eurostile"/>
    <var name="font-numeric" value="JetBrains Mono"/>
  </variables>
  
  <style class="osc-knob">
    <property name="diameter" value="$knob-size-large"/>
    <property name="material" value="brushedAluminum"/>
    <property name="indicatorColor" value="$accent"/>
    <property name="indicatorWidth" value="3"/>
    <property name="rotationRange" value="-135,135"/>
  </style>
  
  <style class="osc-knob:hover">
    <property name="indicatorBrightness" value="1.5"/>
  </style>
  
  <style class="filter-knob">
    <property name="diameter" value="$knob-size-large"/>
    <property name="material" value="anodizedBlack"/>
    <property name="indicatorColor" value="#66ccff"/>
  </style>
  
  <style id="#master-volume">
    <!-- Specific override for one component -->
    <property name="diameter" value="80"/>
  </style>
  
  <style tag="label">
    <!-- Applies to all label elements -->
    <property name="font" value="$font-label"/>
    <property name="size" value="11"/>
    <property name="effect" value="engraved"/>
    <property name="color" value="#888"/>
  </style>
</stylesheet>
```

**Selectors supported**:
- `class="..."` - matches components with that class
- `id="#..."` - matches a specific component
- `tag="..."` - matches all components of a type
- `:hover`, `:active`, `:disabled` - state-based variants
- Combinator: `class="osc-knob filter-knob"` - applies both

**Inheritance**:
- Components inherit from sections
- Sections inherit from panel
- Variables cascade down the hierarchy
- Local properties override class properties override variable defaults

## 2.2 Style Editor in Studio

New panel in the Design workspace: "Stylesheet"

**UI layout**:
- Top: variable list with name/value editors
- Middle: class definitions in a tree view (collapsible)
- Bottom: live preview of the selected class on a sample component

**Features**:
- Click any component in the canvas to see what classes/styles apply to it
- Highlight the source of each property (variable, class, local override)
- "Add class" button - prompt for name, opens editor
- Drag components to apply classes (drop on a class name)
- Right-click → "Extract style" - takes a component's local properties and creates a class from them
- Right-click → "Apply class to similar" - finds all components with similar properties and assigns them the same class

**Live preview**: As the user edits a class, every component using that class updates in real-time, both in the Studio canvas and in the connected JUCE plugin.

## 2.3 Bridge Updates for Stylesheets

The C++ bridge needs to understand stylesheets:

- New message type: `update_stylesheet` carries the full stylesheet
- The bridge re-applies styles to all matching components
- The bridge tracks which properties came from styles vs local overrides
- When a style changes, only style-derived properties get updated (local overrides preserved)

**StyleResolver class**:
```cpp
class StyleResolver {
public:
    void loadStylesheet(const Stylesheet& sheet);
    
    // Compute final properties for a component, applying all matching styles
    PropertySet resolve(const ComponentSpec& component) const;
    
    // Get just the style-derived properties (excluding local overrides)
    PropertySet resolveStyleOnly(const ComponentSpec& component) const;
};
```

## 2.4 Theme Variants

Once stylesheets exist, theme variants become trivial. Add UI for managing themes:

- A plugin can have multiple themes (Dark, Light, Vintage, etc.)
- Each theme is a stylesheet with the same class names but different values
- User picks a default theme
- "Theme switcher" UI in the Studio for previewing
- Generated JUCE code includes theme switching support
- End users of the plugin can switch themes at runtime via a menu

**Auto-variant generation**:
- "Generate light variant from dark" - AI inverts the colors intelligently
- "Generate vintage variant" - applies aged/warm color shifts
- Result is a starting point the user can refine

## 2.5 Style Inspector

When debugging why a component looks the way it does, the user needs visibility:

- Right-click any component → "Inspect Styles"
- Opens a panel showing all applied styles, in order of precedence
- Each property shows its source (variable, class, local, default)
- Click a source to jump to its definition
- Like the Chrome DevTools inspector, but for plugin GUIs

