# MILESTONE 3: Animation Sandbox & Brand System

**Strategic goal**: Two unique features that no competitor offers - an interactive sandbox to test animations before exporting, and a brand consistency system for developers building plugin families.

**Why these together**: Both are about confidence and consistency. The Sandbox lets you trust your animations before committing them. The Brand System lets you trust that your visual identity will stay consistent across plugins.

## 3.1 Animation Sandbox - The Concept

A new workspace (or major panel within Animate workspace) called "Sandbox" that simulates a running plugin without requiring a DAW.

**What it does**:
- Loads the current design as if it were a real plugin
- Components are fully interactive: drag knobs, click buttons, watch meters
- Multiple test signal sources: sine sweeps, white noise, audio file playback, manual MIDI
- Real-time visualization of parameter values
- Can record interactions as GIF or MP4 for documentation

## 3.2 Sandbox Architecture

```
src/sandbox/
├── SandboxRuntime.ts       # Simulates a plugin runtime
├── ParameterEngine.ts      # Tracks and animates parameter values
├── SignalGenerator.ts      # Test signal sources
├── AudioAnalyzer.ts        # Computes meter values from signals
├── MidiSimulator.ts        # Simulates MIDI input
├── InteractionRecorder.ts  # Records user interactions
└── components/
    ├── SandboxCanvas.tsx   # Renders the design interactively
    ├── SignalPanel.tsx     # Source selection + transport
    ├── ParameterMonitor.tsx # Live parameter values display
    └── RecordingControls.tsx
```

## 3.3 Test Signal Sources

The sandbox needs realistic test signals to drive meters and visualizations:

**Built-in generators**:
- Sine sweep (configurable frequency range, duration)
- White noise / pink noise / brown noise
- Pulse train (configurable frequency, width)
- Burst (short attack, decay - tests meter ballistics)
- Chirp (frequency rising over time)
- Dual sine (for stereo)
- Silence (for testing meter rest state)

**Audio file playback**:
- Drag-and-drop WAV/MP3
- Loop / one-shot modes
- Pre/post gain
- Bypass and solo

**MIDI input simulator**:
- On-screen keyboard
- MIDI file playback
- Recording from connected MIDI device (if Web MIDI available)
- Note generation patterns (scales, arpeggios)

## 3.4 Realistic Meter Behavior in Sandbox

Meters in the sandbox must behave EXACTLY as they will in the real plugin:

- Use the same ballistics code that will be exported to JUCE
- Run the test signal through a JS implementation of the same DSP
- For VU meters: 300ms attack/decay (IEC 60268-17)
- For PPM: fast attack, slow decay per BBC/EBU spec
- For digital peak: instant attack, configurable hold/release
- For LUFS: full ITU-R BS.1770 implementation

This is critical: if the sandbox shows different behavior than the real plugin, it's worse than useless.

## 3.5 Knob Physics in Sandbox

Same principle for knobs:
- Use the same physics implementation that gets exported to JUCE
- Mouse drag mirrors what users will experience
- Inertia, detents, fine control - all working
- Visual feedback exactly matches the live plugin behavior

## 3.6 Recording & Export

Convert interactions into shareable artifacts:

- **GIF export**: Record N seconds of interaction → optimized GIF
- **MP4 export**: Higher quality, with audio if signal sources include audio
- **Frame sequence export**: PNG frames for use in documentation
- **Interaction script export**: JSON of all events, can be replayed deterministically

**Use cases**:
- Marketing materials for the plugin
- Documentation showing how a feature works
- Bug reports ("here's exactly what I did")
- Onboarding/tutorial recordings

