## 5.5 Light Bleed and Bloom Effects

Subtle effects that separate good from great:

**LED bloom**:
- Bright LEDs create soft halos around themselves
- Halo color matches LED color
- Halo intensity follows LED brightness
- Color bleeds slightly into nearby surfaces
- Implements proper HDR bloom with pre-filter, downsample, upsample, composite

**Backlit displays**:
- LCD/OLED backlight bleeds slightly outside the screen frame
- Color temperature affects nearby surfaces
- Subtle illumination of screws and bezels near the screen

**Phosphor persistence (CRT mode)**:
- Bright pixels fade slowly, not instantly
- Realistic CRT scanlines
- Slight barrel distortion at edges
- Color separation at high contrast edges

**Tube glow (vacuum tubes)**:
- Realistic warmup curve
- Orange/amber glow that pulses subtly
- Heat distortion above the tube (subtle)

## 5.6 Subpixel-Accurate Rendering

Plugins need to look crisp at every scale:

- Sub-pixel positioning support throughout
- Pixel snapping where appropriate (text, sharp edges)
- Anti-aliased edges everywhere
- Multi-sample supersampling for high-quality renders
- Bilinear / bicubic / Lanczos filtering options for textures

**Scale-aware rendering**:
- Detect target DPI
- Adjust line widths to maintain visual consistency
- Snap text baselines to pixel grid
- Adjust shadow offsets to remain crisp

## 5.7 SVG-First Pipeline

Where possible, render components from vectors instead of bitmaps:

**Conversion logic**:
- Foundry-generated knobs can be exported as SVG when materials allow
- SVG rendering at runtime in JUCE (via JUCE's path support)
- Fall back to bitmap when material complexity requires it
- Hybrid: SVG base + bitmap details overlay

**Benefits**:
- Resolution independent (perfect at any DPI)
- Smaller binary data
- Faster runtime rendering
- Easier theme variations (color changes don't require re-baking)

